1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,360
Lesson 22: real terrain, DEM profile, locator map, traffic directions and height scan

2
00:00:06,410 --> 00:00:18,915
YA-NEC course, lesson 22: real terrain, in the style of N6BV's HFTA. From the Examples we open the ARRL 5-element Yagi for 20 m, at 21 m over average ground.

3
00:00:18,965 --> 00:00:30,409
Patterns tab: since 1.2 the band at the top has six items. We choose Real terrain.

4
00:00:30,459 --> 00:00:40,972
On the left the antenna position: a locator of 8 to 12 characters, here JN70CN82WE. Below: latitude, longitude and ground height from the DEM.

5
00:00:41,022 --> 00:00:51,682
Pick on the map: aerial photo with the locator grid, which gets finer as you zoom in.

6
00:00:51,732 --> 00:00:59,482
A click picks the point; on the right the 10-character locator, the coordinates and the ground height.

7
00:00:59,532 --> 00:01:06,557
Use this point: back to the page with the new locator.

8
00:01:06,607 --> 00:01:17,091
Terrain profile from the DEM: INGV's TINITALY, 10 m cells; outside Italy SRTM1. It is downloaded on first use and stays in the data/dem folder.

9
00:01:17,141 --> 00:01:25,624
By traffic direction: six paths, with azimuth and distance recomputed from the locator. First the USA east coast.

10
00:01:25,674 --> 00:01:36,691
At the top the profile towards the USA, with the reflection point. Below the vertical lobe: blue over the real terrain, dashed orange over flat ground.

11
00:01:36,741 --> 00:01:51,028
On the right the HFTA-style results. Figure of merit on the path: -0.8 dB compared with flat ground. But at low angles the real terrain gives more than 10 dB extra: the coast drops towards the sea.

12
00:01:51,078 --> 00:01:59,409
Now Japan: another profile. Figure of merit +1.9 dB, first maximum at 2 degrees instead of 13.

13
00:01:59,459 --> 00:02:06,343
Antenna height scan: the calculation is repeated from 6 to 30 m. Meanwhile, how it works.

14
00:02:06,393 --> 00:02:20,079
The antenna pattern comes from NEC; each facet of the profile reflects with the Fresnel coefficients, weighted by the part of the Fresnel zone it covers; over the ridges there is diffraction.

15
00:02:20,129 --> 00:02:26,711
Self-test: over a flat profile the result matches NEC within 0.1 dB.

16
00:02:26,761 --> 00:02:36,578
Here is the result of the scan: for Japan the best height is 14 m, with the average gain over the useful angles from 1 to 15 degrees.

17
00:02:36,628 --> 00:02:48,821
The terrain matters as much as the antenna. Everything is explained in chapter 23 of the manual. Next lesson: reception, tables and tolerances. 73 de IC8TEM
